WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.590
Logan is a more or less self-contained entry
in the X-Men franchise with director James

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:11.179
Mangold saying he set it in 2029 around 5
or 6 years after the events of Days of Future

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:15.799
Past to ensure he didn't have to worry too
much about X-Men movie continuity.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:20.460
However, Logan still teases numerous connections
to the X-Men cinematic universe and drops

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.350
some nice easter eggs to the comic books.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:26.740
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video, I'm going to unpack some

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:31.210
of the best easter eggs and X-Men references
in Hugh Jackman's last movie as Wolverine.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.530
I've also got an awesome set of Logan merchandise
to give away which includes a T-shirt, shot

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:39.149
glasses, a dog-tag bottle opener and a metal
notebook.

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:43.159
For a chance to win, all you need to do is
subscribe and leave a comment about the easter

00:00:43.159 --> 00:00:45.450
eggs or let me know about any I missed.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.829
And for bonus ways to enter, check out the
Gleam link in the video description below.

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:53.609
Quick warning there will be spoilers ahead
if you haven't seen the movie yet, so if that's

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:57.579
you, check out my spoiler-free review of Logan
and come back when you've had a chance to

00:00:57.579 --> 00:00:59.410
see the movie!

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:03.690
Wolverine's healing factor isn't working properly
in Logan, he's aging and he just doesn't look

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.909
like the same Wolverine we're used to seeing
in the X-Men movies.

00:01:06.909 --> 00:01:11.420
Still, there's an amusing shout-out to Hugh
Jackman's signature Wolverine look when the

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:15.030
mutant children trim Logan's thick beard while
he's asleep and give him back some of that

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.450
distinctive Wolverine look we're used to from
the movies!

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.380
And there's also a fun little nod to Wolverine's
love of cigars when he grabs a handful of

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.100
them just before leaving the gas station.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.899
The Statue of Liberty which was centre stage
during the climactic battle at the end of

00:01:28.899 --> 00:01:33.780
the first X-Men movie is referenced a couple
of times; first via the Liberty Motel where

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.229
Laura and her guardian Gabriela Lopez are
staying, and secondly during a conversation

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:43.079
between Charles and Logan who discuss the
events at Liberty Island from the first movie.

00:01:43.079 --> 00:01:47.210
Logan builds on the post-credits scene from
X-Men: Apocalypse where we saw dark suited

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:51.790
men from Essex Corp walking in to the Weapon
X facility at Alkali Lake to collect various

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.729
blood samples being stored there.

00:01:53.729 --> 00:01:57.700
In Logan, there's a company called Alkali
Transigen which has taken up the Weapon X

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.950
program creating new young mutants like X-23.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.729
The name Alkali Transigen could be a double
reference to both Alkali Lake where the Weapon

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:10.009
X program started and Transigen could be a
nod to Terrigen Mist.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:13.920
Terrigen is a substance from the comics that
when it was released into the Earth's atmosphere

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.190
slowly started killing off mutants.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:20.900
And there's a curious parallel in the movie
when Zander Rice explains how Alkali Transigen

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:26.010
distributed gene therapy through soda and
breakfast cereals to almost eradicate random

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:27.010
mutations.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:31.980
Rice's plan wasn't to exterminate all mutants,
but to eradicate any mutants that they didn't

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.011
develop and control themselves.

00:02:33.011 --> 00:02:37.810
By the way, Rice's virus-like gene therapy
could also provide an explanation for why

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.319
Logan's mutant healing factor has been waning.

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:45.769
By the way, Zander Rice is the lead scientist
responsible for creating X-23, just like he

00:02:45.769 --> 00:02:47.390
is in the comics.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:51.400
And in Logan we also learn that Wolverine
was responsible for killing Rice's father,

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.040
which also happened in the comics, when he
went beserker during his escape from Weapon

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.330
X in X-Men: Apocalypse.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:01.610
When Logan flips through Transigen case files
at the hotel, we see notes revealing some

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:06.930
of the source DNA for the young mutants including
DNA from James Howlett, aka Logan, which was

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.970
used for X-23.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:12.900
Another case file reveals that one of the
sources of DNA is Christopher Bradley who

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.530
appeared in X-Men Origins: Wolverine and was
one of the original Team X members.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.819
Bradley was able to control electrical devices,
but left the team until he was hunted down

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:25.750
by Sabretooth to retrieve his DNA for the
Weapon XI program.

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:30.340
In Logan, we find out that Professor X has
gone into hiding and has to take regular medication

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.459
to prevent seizures that can cause catastrophe
all around him.

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:37.819
And at one point in the movie, a radio report
mentions the Westchester incident which occurred

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:41.080
a year previously in which various mutants
were killed.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:45.239
Westchester is the location of the X-Mansion
and there's a heavy implication that Charles

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:48.599
accidentally lost control of his powers killing
many of the X-Men.

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:54.129
This is a nod to the Old Man Logan comic storyline
which Logan loosely adapts where it was Wolverine

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:57.989
who accidentally slaughtered the X-Men after
being tricked by the villain Mysterio.

00:03:57.989 --> 00:04:02.840
By the way, there are also some cool visual
nods to that comic series such as this shot

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:06.830
of Logan punching a guy all the way through
his skull with his claws.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:10.420
And there's another picture of Hugh Jackman's
Logan looking grizzled and worn with a couple

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:14.689
of chest wounds which is evocative of this
look in Old Man Logan.

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:19.040
Another comic book series which heavily influenced
the Logan movie is "Death of Wolverine".

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.440
In the comic book series, Wolverine also meets
his end as he rescues a group of mutants that

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.220
are being experimented on by Doctor Cornelius,
a senior member of the Weapon X program.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:32.870
And in Logan, the young mutants' hideout is
a location called Eden, a name that has echoes

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:37.039
of the Paradise base that Logan rescues them
from in Death of Wolverine.

00:04:37.039 --> 00:04:42.230
Death of Wolverine also features a Logan whose
healing factor has stopped working properly,

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:46.320
and when Wolverine uses a serum on himself
at the end of the movie, it's a nod to how

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.389
he gave the mutants he rescued in the comic
a regenerative serum.

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:55.039
By the way, some of the mutants that Logan
and X-23 meet up with at Eden hint towards

00:04:55.039 --> 00:04:57.130
possible future X-Force members.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.940
There's Rictor, an original X-Force member
in the comics with extremely powerful seismic

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:06.220
abilities which, in the movie at one point,
he uses against X-24.

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:11.169
And another young mutant, Gideon, is an X-Force
mutant villain in the comics.

00:05:11.169 --> 00:05:15.810
Although Wolverine's comic book canary costume
was teased in the alternate ending to James

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.860
Mangold's movie The Wolverine, we don't actually
get to see it in Logan where it would have

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.850
probably felt out of place in terms of the
tone set by the movie.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:26.970
There's still a cool easter egg to the costume
at the very end of the movie during Logan's

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:31.449
funeral when one of the mutant children is
holding a Wolverine toy dressed in the canary

00:05:31.449 --> 00:05:32.620
costume.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:38.060
The costume also appears earlier via the X-Men
comics that Logan finds Laura is reading.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:41.710
The X-Men comics that Laura was reading were
made especially for the movie, however one

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:46.940
of the issue numbers is issue 132, which could
hint to the real comic book Uncanny X-Men

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:51.490
#132 where Wolverine meets Donald Pierce for
the first time.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:56.099
As they fight in the comic, Wolverine discovers
Donald Pierce's cybernetic arm which of course

00:05:56.099 --> 00:05:58.180
he also has in the Logan movie.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.990
And Logan's comments, as he flips through
the comics, are an interesting bit of commentary

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:05.520
on the status of superheroes in this part
of the X-Men universe.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.130
When Logan says that "Maybe a quarter of it
happened.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.490
And not like this", he's commenting on how
he's tired of heroes being mythologised and

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:16.280
turned into celebrities whose stories are
constantly retold and distorted.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.710
And it could also be a little side swipe at
the X-Men movie franchise and how it's become

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:24.770
known for messing around with the timelines
which end up wiping away the events of whole

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:29.480
movie trilogies and introducing new continuity
problems at the same time.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:34.080
Nevertheless, Logan still includes a little
homage to how Fox like to sprinkle X symbols

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.770
into the X-Men movies when Laura turns the
cross on Wolverine's grave on to its side

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.349
so that it resembles an X.

00:06:41.349 --> 00:06:44.639
Mangold's Logan also channels several non-superhero
movies.

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:49.130
A couple of particular note are the road movie
Little Miss Sunshine starring Abigail Breslin

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:54.440
and the c western Shane, which features a
gunslinger hero who wants to leave his past

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:55.440
behind.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:59.889
Shane gets various shout-outs in the film
and it's clear that Mangold feels a real connection

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:04.440
between the story of Shane and the legend
of Logan, including Logan's desire for a simple

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.509
life and weariness with his life as a hero,
as well as the effect violence has had on

00:07:08.509 --> 00:07:10.940
Logan's life and the lives of those he loves.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:16.229
By the way, at one point we see Wolverine's
dog tags that he was given in X-Men Origins:

00:07:16.229 --> 00:07:20.250
Wolverine, just before undergoing the adamantium
bonding procedure.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:24.710
Wolverine actually tossed his dog tags away
at the end of X-Men 2 when he abandoned Stryker

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.910
next to the collapsing dam at Alkali Lake.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:31.129
Those dog tags would have been washed away
at the end of X-Men 2 so how Wolverine has

00:07:31.129 --> 00:07:35.509
them in Logan is presumably because he changed
the timeline in Days of Future Past or they're

00:07:35.509 --> 00:07:37.750
simply a different version of those tags.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:42.620
And there's a nod to James Mangold's The Wolverine
movie via a samurai sword that you can spot

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.639
in Logan and Caliban's home.

00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:48.800
It could be the same katana that Yashida gives
Logan in that movie after he saves him during

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.300
the bombing of Nagasaki in World War II or
a similar samurai sword that hints to Wolverine's

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.960
connection to Japan and samurai culture.

